[Core_PDML_demo

    This is a valid PDML document showing examples of storing different types of data using
    only Core PDML — PDML extensions are not used.

    You can find more examples (with explanations) in the article "Core PDML Examples",
    available here: https://pdml-lang.dev/docs/core/examples/index.html

    [string_value Some arbitrary string]
    [integer_value 123]
    [boolean_value true]
    [date_value 2026-05-26]
    [null_value]
    [text
        Some arbitrary text
        Line 2
        Line 3
    ]

    [string_list
        [el string 1]
        [el string 2]
        [el string 3]
    ]
    Alternative:
    [string_list
        [_ string 1]
        [_ string 2]
        [_ string 3]
    ]
    [any_type_list
        [el some string]
        [el 123]
        [el]
        [el [leaf_child][text_child text]]
    ]

    [string_string_map
        [key_1 value 1]
        [key_2 value 2]
        [key_3 value 3]
    ]
    [string_any_type_map
        [string some string]
        [null_entry]
        [complex_entry [leaf_child][text_child text]]
    ]

    [record
        [field_1 value_1]
        [field_2 value_2]
        [field_3]
        [field_4
            [child_41]
            [child_42 some value]
        ]
    ]

    [tree
        [child_1]
        [child_2
            [child_21]
            [child_22 [child_221][child_222]]
        ]
    ]
    Same tree, using compact form:
    [tree [child_1][child_2 [child_21][child_22 [child_221][child_222]]]]

    [markup You can write text in [b bold], [i italic], or [b [i bold and italic]].]

    Character escape sequences in text:
    [note The following characters must be escaped: \\, \[, \], and \^]

    Character escape sequences in tag:
    [net\sweight\s\[kg\] 177]

    You can use any case-convention in PDML documents, such as:
    [kebab-case-example]
    [snake_case_example]
    [camelCaseExample]
    [dot.case.example]
    [path/case/example]
]
